---
import Studio from "../../layouts/Studio.astro";
import RelatedPosts from "../../components/RelatedPosts.astro";
import { SITE, DEFAULT_OG, CALENDLY_URL, fmtDate, iso, POSTS } from "../../lib/blog.mjs";

const slug = "squarespace-vs-custom-website";
const idx = POSTS.findIndex((p) => p.slug === slug);
const post = POSTS[idx];

const canonical = `${SITE}/blog/${slug}/`;
const ogImage = post.coverImage ? `${SITE}${post.coverImage}` : DEFAULT_OG;
const primaryTag = post.tags[0] || "Notes";
const readMins = post.readMins;
const showUpdated = post.updatedDate && +new Date(post.updatedDate) > +new Date(post.pubDate);
const updatedLabel = showUpdated ? `Updated ${fmtDate(post.updatedDate)}` : null;

const jsonld = JSON.stringify({
  "@context": "https://schema.org",
  "@graph": [
    {
      "@type": "BlogPosting",
      "@id": `${canonical}#post`,
      headline: post.title,
      description: post.description,
      datePublished: iso(post.pubDate),
      dateModified: iso(post.updatedDate || post.pubDate),
      author: { "@type": "Person", name: "Divyansh Sood", url: `${SITE}/`, "@id": `${SITE}/#person` },
      publisher: {
        "@type": "Organization",
        "@id": `${SITE}/#studio`,
        name: "Divyansh Sood® Studio",
        logo: { "@type": "ImageObject", url: `${SITE}/icon.svg` },
      },
      isPartOf: { "@type": "WebSite", "@id": `${SITE}/#website` },
      mainEntityOfPage: { "@type": "WebPage", "@id": canonical },
      image: ogImage,
      articleSection: primaryTag,
      keywords: post.tags.join(", "),
      wordCount: post.wordCount,
      timeRequired: `PT${readMins}M`,
      speakable: {
        "@type": "SpeakableSpecification",
        cssSelector: [".post-title", ".post-lead"],
      },
      inLanguage: "en",
      url: canonical,
    },
    {
      "@type": "BreadcrumbList",
      itemListElement: [
        { "@type": "ListItem", position: 1, name: "Home", item: `${SITE}/` },
        { "@type": "ListItem", position: 2, name: "Blog", item: `${SITE}/blog/` },
        { "@type": "ListItem", position: 3, name: post.title, item: canonical },
      ],
    },
  ],
});
---

<Studio
  title={post.seoTitle ?? `${post.title} · Divyansh Sood`}
  description={post.description}
  canonical={canonical}
  keywords={post.tags.join(", ")}
  ogType="article"
  ogTitle={post.title}
  ogImage={ogImage}
  ogImageW={1200}
  ogImageH={750}
  articlePublished={iso(post.pubDate)}
  articleModified={iso(post.updatedDate || post.pubDate)}
  tags={post.tags}
  jsonld={jsonld}
>
  <main>
    <header class="post-hero">
      <div class="post-hero-bg" aria-hidden="true"></div>
      <div class="wrap narrow">
        <div class="eyebrow">
          <span>{primaryTag}</span>
          <span>{fmtDate(post.pubDate)}</span>
          {updatedLabel && <span class="post-updated">{updatedLabel}</span>}
          <span>{readMins} min read</span>
        </div>
        <h1 class="post-title">{post.title}</h1>
        <p class="post-lead">{post.description}</p>
        <div class="post-meta">
          <span>By Divyansh Sood</span><span>{fmtDate(post.pubDate)}</span><span>{readMins} min read</span>
        </div>
      </div>
    </header>

    {post.coverImage && (
      <div class="wrap narrow">
        <figure class="post-cover">
          <img src={post.coverImage} alt={post.title} width="1200" height="750" fetchpriority="high" decoding="async" />
        </figure>
      </div>
    )}

    <article class="wrap narrow post-body">
<p>Squarespace made a specific promise and kept it: anyone can have a website that doesn’t embarrass them. For a photographer, a café, a therapist — that promise is genuinely enough.</p>
<p>But there’s a moment when it stops being enough, and most owners can’t name it until it’s cost them a year. This post is about naming that moment.</p>
<hr>
<h2 id="what-squarespace-is-actually-good-at">What Squarespace is actually good at</h2>
<ul>
<li><strong>Getting you live this week.</strong> Pick a template, drop in photos, publish. No developer, no invoices.</li>
<li><strong>Looking tasteful by default.</strong> The templates are genuinely well designed. You can’t make a truly ugly Squarespace site without effort.</li>
<li><strong>Being one bill.</strong> Hosting, SSL, forms, a basic store — one subscription, no moving parts.</li>
</ul>
<p>If your website is a business card — hours, photos, contact — stay on Squarespace. This is not that story.</p>
<hr>
<h2 id="the-moment-it-stops-being-enough">The moment it stops being enough</h2>
<p><strong>You start competing on search.</strong> Squarespace covers SEO basics — titles, descriptions, a sitemap. But the layer where competitive queries get won is out of reach: full structured-data graphs, per-page schema, edge redirects, an llms.txt for AI crawlers, load-speed tuning past the platform’s runtime. When a competitor’s custom site and your template compete for “best boutique stays in your town,” the ceiling is real.</p>
<p><strong>You start looking like your category, not your brand.</strong> Templates converge. Your customers have seen your layout before — on other sites, with other logos. A premium offer inside a familiar template quietly reads as a standard offer. Design freedom inside Squarespace exists, but it’s decoration on someone else’s structure.</p>
<p><strong>You start selling seriously.</strong> Squarespace commerce works until you want what it doesn’t do: a checkout that matches your brand end to end, drops and limited runs, India-friendly payments like UPI, subscriptions with custom logic. Each workaround is another plugin, another fee, another seam.</p>
<p><strong>You do the three-year math.</strong> The subscription looks small monthly. Multiply it out, add the commerce transaction fees, and compare it against a custom site you pay for once and own outright — including the code, which moves with you to any host. I’ve written up that math in <a href="/blog/the-real-cost-of-renting-your-storefront/">the real cost of renting your storefront</a>.</p>
<hr>
<h2 id="what-custom-actually-buys-you">What custom actually buys you</h2>
<p>Not “fancier design.” Control at the three layers Squarespace can’t open:</p>
<ul>
<li><strong>Speed.</strong> Static, custom-coded pages carry no platform runtime. That’s felt on phones and measured by Google.</li>
<li><strong>Search.</strong> Every technical SEO and GEO lever is yours — which is where organic categories get won in 2026.</li>
<li><strong>Ownership.</strong> The site is an asset. You can move it, extend it into a web app, hand it to a future team. Nobody can raise your rent.</li>
</ul>
<p>A rebuild I shipped for <a href="/projects/nandini/">a local taxi operator</a> replaced exactly this kind of ceiling: the founder’s verdict was that it looks ten times more premium than competitors — and bookings that used to leak away now come through the site.</p>
<hr>
<h2 id="the-honest-decision-rule">The honest decision rule</h2>
<ul>
<li>Website is a business card, budget is tight → <strong>Squarespace</strong>, without guilt.</li>
<li>Search is (or should be) a customer channel, or the brand is the product → <strong>custom</strong>.</li>
<li>Selling premium products through a template → custom, sooner than you think — the template is costing you more than the rebuild.</li>
</ul>
<p>The wrong move is the middle one: paying for Squarespace for years <em>while</em> wishing it did more. Decide which site you’re running, then run it fully.</p>
<hr>
<p><em>I’m Divyansh Sood. I build custom-coded, conversion-first websites and <a href="/services/landing-page-design/">landing pages</a> — fast, findable, and yours to own. If you’re comparing builders more broadly, I’ve also written up <a href="/blog/framer-vs-custom-code-2026/">Framer vs custom code</a> and <a href="/blog/custom-website-vs-wordpress/">custom vs WordPress</a>. Send me your Squarespace site at <a href="mailto:hello@divyanshsood.com">hello@divyanshsood.com</a> and I’ll tell you honestly whether you’ve hit the ceiling.</em></p>
      <div class="tags">{post.tags.map((t) => <span>{t}</span>)}</div>
    </article>

    <RelatedPosts slug={slug} audience={post.audience} />

    <section class="post-foot-cta">
      <div class="wrap narrow">
        {post.audience === "indian-smb" ? (
          <>
            <h2>Working on something in India?</h2>
            <p>If a post here matches what you're building, send me the brief. WhatsApp reply in under an hour, 9 AM–9 PM IST — I'll tell you within a day if it's a fit.</p>
            <div class="cta-row">
              <a class="btn btn-primary" href="https://wa.me/919816091875" target="_blank" rel="noopener"
                >Message me on WhatsApp <span aria-hidden="true">↗</span></a
              >
              <a class="btn btn-ghost" href="/#work">See the work</a>
            </div>
          </>
        ) : post.audience === "d2c-brand" ? (
          <>
            <h2>Building a brand a theme can't carry?</h2>
            <p>If your store has a model that off-the-shelf builders fight — hand-numbered drops, creator commerce, premium D2C — tell me about it. I read every brief personally.</p>
            <div class="cta-row">
              <a class="btn btn-primary" href="mailto:hello@divyanshsood.com"
                >Email me <span aria-hidden="true">↗</span></a
              >
              <a class="btn btn-ghost" href="/#work">See the work</a>
            </div>
          </>
        ) : (
          <>
            <h2>Working on something similar?</h2>
            <p>If a post here matches what you're building, send me the brief. I reply personally, usually within a couple of hours — and I'll tell you within a day if it's a fit.</p>
            <div class="cta-row">
              <a class="btn btn-primary" href="mailto:hello@divyanshsood.com"
                >Email me <span aria-hidden="true">↗</span></a
              >
              <a class="btn btn-ghost" href={CALENDLY_URL} target="_blank" rel="noopener"
                >Book a 15-min intro <span aria-hidden="true">↗</span></a
              >
            </div>
          </>
        )}
      </div>
    </section>
  </main>
</Studio>
